[19.883432, "o", "R1_ARM=PC R1_ENTRY=coordinator R1_ROUTES=coordinator=cloud,implementor=local,verifier=local,plan=cloud,implement=local,critique=local,smol=local M2_LOCAL_REPLAY=false\r\n"]
[301.113523, "o", "  ⚙ 3 steps folded · note ×3 · nothing changed  /expand\r\n      from  \r\n  [tools] Stopped: the same tool call repeated 3 times without progress, after 27 call(s).\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — wrapping up\r\n  ⚙ task \r\n    ⚠ task verifier — 27 tool call(s)\r\n  deepseek-v4.1-flash — 16 tool call(s) so far\r\n  │ The verifier role is barred from invoking any Python interpreter by its o…\r\n[approval] delegate to \"implementor\" — approved\r\n[approval] bash: pip --version 2>&1 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash pip --version 2>&1\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 1 tool call(s) so far\r\n[approval] bash: pip install --dry-run --no-input --disable-pip-version-check --report - packaging 2>&1 | tail -12 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash pip install --dry-run --no-input --disable-pip-version-check --report …\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 2 tool call(s) so far\r\n[approval] bash: python3 -m pip config list 2>&1; echo \"---\"; cat /etc/pip.conf ~/.config/pip/pip.conf ~/.pip/pip.conf 2>&1 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash python3"]
[301.113558, "o", " -m pip config list 2>&1; echo \"---\"; cat /etc/pip.conf ~/.conf…\r\n    ✗ bash — exit 1\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 3 tool call(s) so far\r\n  ⚙ task \r\n    ✓ task implementor — 3 tool call(s)\r\n  deepseek-v4.1-flash — 17 tool call(s) so far\r\nFixed.\r\n\r\n**What was wrong.** Not your Python installation as such — the `pip` *package source* was missing. Your default `python3` is `/usr/local/bin/python3` (Python 3.13.1, on Debian 12), and `/usr/local/bin/pip`, `pip3` and `pip3.13` were perfectly good launcher scripts. But `/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/` held only the `pip-24.3.1.dist-info` metadata directory with no `.py` files behind it. So every entry point — bare `pip` and `python3 -m pip` alike — died with `No module named pip`. That is why it looked like a broken installation rather than a missing package: the command existed, the thing it imported did not.\r\n\r\nA second trap was sitting underneath: `/usr/lib/python3.11/EXTERNALLY-MANAGED` is present, so the OS Python 3.11 is PEP 668-locked. Had"]
[301.113562, "o", " you fallen back to `/usr/bin/python3 -m pip install`, you would have hit a *different* refusal — `externally-managed-environment` — and concluded the same thing twice for unrelated reasons.\r\n\r\n**The repair.** `python3 -m ensurepip --upgrade` on its own did nothing: it saw the orphaned `dist-info` and reported \"Requirement already satisfied\" without restoring any files. Removing that stale `pip-24.3.1.dist-info` directory and re-running `ensurepip --upgrade` installed the bundled wheel cleanly (`Successfully installed pip-24.3.1`). No `get-pip.py`, no hand-edits under `/usr/local`, no system Python 3.11 touched.\r\n\r\n**Verified, three ways:** `pip 24.3.1 from /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip (python 3.13)`; a throwaway venv installed and imported `six`; and the bare `pip install --dry-run packaging` resolved through to `Would install packaging-26.3`, so index access and resolution both work. No `pip.conf` anywhere, so no stray index-url or proxy.\r\n\r\nTwo things worth knowing:\r\n\r\n- Prefer `python3 "]
[301.11378, "o", "-m venv .venv` for real projects. `/usr/local` is a shared interpreter, and a half-deleted package there is exactly the failure you just spent a while on. The venv test passing means you have a clean route regardless.\r\n- One check I could not complete as intended: I sent an independent verifier role to re-test, and it was blocked from the start — that role's `bash_deny` in `.gnomon/roles.toml` refuses `python3?` outright, so it could not run a single Python command. Its failure says nothing about your machine; the repair was confirmed directly instead. If you rely on that verifier for Python projects, that deny pattern will make it useless for anything but a test runner named `pytest`.\r\n\r\n[refusal] role=coordinator model=deepseek-v4.1-flash tools=17 surface=0d2e5ba66dce\r\n"]
